00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:04.300
a story that hits close to home for many Canadian investors. Imagine your friend offers to manage

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:10.500
your TFSA and you transfer $75,000 into the mutual funds and products he recommends.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:15.520
The orders fill, everything looks fine on the statement, but what you don't see is that your

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:22.340
friend has an undisclosed revenue-sharing agreement that pays him 0.85% annually on certain funds.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:27.380
Similar low-cost ETFs that would have done the same or better job paid him nothing.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:35.300
That 0.85% might not sound like much, but it compounds to roughly $6.375 over five years on a

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:39.880
$75,000 account, money that came directly out of your compounded return.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:45.800
The mechanism is called revenue-sharing or trailer fees. The manufacturer of the financial product

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.540
pays the advisor an ongoing percentage, which creates a clear incentive to recommend higher fee products

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:57.320
even when lower-cost alternatives with identical or better benchmarks exist. What most retail investors

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:02.260
don't realize is that these payments are often not clearly itemized on their statements and can

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:07.340
continue for decades after the initial sale. That's why I always suggest asking three direct

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:12.480
questions before handing over any capital. Are you a fiduciary? Do you receive any revenue-sharing

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.540
or trailer fees on the products you recommend? And can you show me the exact MER and compensation

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:22.380
structure on every holding? The biggest mistake people make is assuming that friendship or

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.700
familiarity removes conflicts of interest. Instead, always demand full disclosure in writing and

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:34.200
compare the net of fees expected return against a simple low-cost ETF portfolio in the same asset classes.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:39.000
Protecting your capital from hidden fees is one of the easiest ways to improve your long-term

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.320
